{"cuisine/egyptian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/egyptian-cuisine","n":"Egyptian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Egypt","intro":[["Egyptian cuisine makes heavy use of poultry, legumes, vegetables and fruit from Egypt's rich Nile Valley and Delta. Examples of Egyptian dishes include rice-stuffed vegetables and grape leaves, hummus, falafel,  shawarma, kebab and kofta. Others include  ful medames, mashed fava beans; koshary, lentils and pasta; and molokhiyya, jute leaf stew."],["A local type of pita known as eish baladi is a staple of Egyptian cuisine, and cheesemaking in Egypt dates back to the First Dynasty of Egypt, with Domiati being the most popular type of cheese consumed today."],["Egyptian cuisine relies heavily on vegetables and legumes, but can also feature meats, most commonly rabbit and poultry such as squab, chicken, duck, quail and goose. Lamb and beef are commonly used in Egyptian cuisine, particularly for grilling and in a variety of stews and traditional dishes. Goat and camel are also eaten but are not as readily available nationwide. Offal is also a popular street food, often served in sandwiches. Fish and seafood are widely consumed across Egypt, with coastal regions such as Alexandria, Suez and Port Said being especially known for their seafood cuisine. Freshwater tilapia and mullet are the most popular types of fish in the country."],["A significant portion of Egyptian cuisine is vegetarian, largely due to the country's agricultural landscape and the farmers historical food traditions. The fertile banks of the Nile River are primarily used for cultivating crops rather than animal grazing, as arable land is limited and livestock farming requires extensive resources such as land, water and fodder. Additionally, the dietary practices of Christians during Lent, who observe religious restrictions that mandate an essentially vegan diet for extended periods of the year, further contribute to the prominence of plant-based dishes in Egyptian cuisine."],["Tea is the national drink of Egypt and mint is a popular addition, while beer is the most common alcoholic beverage. Although Islam is the majority faith in Egypt and practicing Muslims tend to avoid alcohol, alcoholic drinks are still readily available in the country. Karkadeh is also a very popular traditional beverage in Egypt, served both cold and hot."],["Popular desserts in Egypt include baqlawa, basbousa, kunafa and qatayef. Common ingredients in desserts include dates, honey, and almonds."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"egyptian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Chez Nermine","ref":"site/cheznermine-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cheznermine.com","note":"Egyptian recipes and the stories behind them from Egyptian American cook Nermine.","url":"https://cheznermine.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Amira's Pantry","ref":"site/amiraspantry-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"amiraspantry.com","note":"Egyptian section of Amira's recipe blog, with koshari, hawawshi, feteer and sweets.","url":"https://amiraspantry.com/tag/egyptian-cuisine/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Eat, Habibi, Eat!: Fresh Recipes for Modern Egyptian Cooking","ref":"cookbook/eat-habibi-eat-fresh-recipes-for-modern-egyptian-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Shahir Massoud, 2021","note":"Modern Egyptian home cooking from a Canadian chef and TV cook.","url":"https://penguinrandomhouse.ca/books/617268/eat-habibi-eat-by-shahir-massoud/9780525610939","suggested":true},{"name":"The Taste of Egypt: Home Cooking from the Middle East","ref":"cookbook/the-taste-of-egypt-home-cooking-from-the-middle-east","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Dyna Eldaief","note":"Egyptian family recipes, published by AUC Press.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781649034281","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Pistachio nut","ingredient/pistachio-nut"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"],["Zucchini","ingredient/zucchini"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/15/Egyptian_Spices_01.jpg/500px-Egyptian_Spices_01.jpg","w":500,"h":604,"by":"Dina Said","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_Spices_01.jpg"}},"cuisine/yemeni-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yemeni-cuisine","n":"Yemeni cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Yemen","intro":[["Yemeni cuisine is distinct from the wider Middle Eastern cuisines with regional variation. Despite being an Arab country, Yemeni cooking traditions have been influenced more by Indian and Indonesian methods as a result of its maritime history."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yemeni-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yemeni_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Sifratna: Recipes from Our Yemeni Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/sifratna-recipes-from-our-yemeni-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Amjaad Al-Hussain, 2018","note":"Yemeni family recipes compiled by a Virginia-based Yemeni-American author.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Khaliat nahl","dish/khaliat-nahl"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/68/200612_Yemen-55_%28353668684%29.jpg/500px-200612_Yemen-55_%28353668684%29.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Franco Pecchio from Milano, Italy","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:200612_Yemen-55_(353668684).jpg"}},"cuisine/nuu-chah-nulth-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nuu-chah-nulth-foodways","n":"Nuu-chah-nulth foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, West coast of Vancouver Island, British Columbia","names":"Nuu-chah-nulth, including Hesquiaht, Toquaht, Ahousaht and Tseshaht","intro":[["Along the west coast of Vancouver Island, ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," is the most important fish, smoked on sticks to keep through the year. When ",["Herring","ingredient/herring"]," spawn, cedar branches are sunk near the spawning grounds and the eggs that coat them are peeled off and eaten. Roe on kelp is gathered too, and whole herring are smoked with head and tail on. Hesquiaht's name comes from eating herring roe off eelgrass."],["Clams, shellfish and sea mammals come from the shore, deer from inland, and berries, shoots and ",["Camas","ingredient/camassia"]," bulbs are gathered in spring and summer. The west coast of Vancouver Island has had no commercial herring fishery since 2005; it's open only to First Nations food, social and ceremonial harvests."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Ha-Shilth-Sa (Nuu-chah-nulth Tribal Council), Herring integral to B.C.'s ecological health and Nuu-chah-nulth traditions","url":"https://mail.hashilthsa.com/node/17060"},{"name":"Toquaht Nation, Our culture","url":"https://toquaht.ca/our-culture"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nuu-chah-nulth-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/wampanoag-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/wampanoag-foodways","n":"Wampanoag foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Southeastern Massachusetts, Cape Cod and the islands","names":"Wampanoag","intro":[["Wampanoag gardens in southeastern New England grow ",["corn","ingredient/maize"],", beans and squash together, and the first green corn comes in midsummer. Corn is cooked into ",["Nasaump","dish/nasaump"],", a thick, filling dish sometimes made with nuts and berries. The shore gives ",["clams","ingredient/clam"],", quahogs, oysters, mussels and lobsters, rivers and the sea give herring, cod, bass and eels, and deer is hunted with every part put to use."],["Giving thanks for food mattered to the Wampanoag and still does. Mashpee Wampanoag chef Sherry Pocknett, the first Indigenous woman to win a James Beard Award, cooks quahog chowder, three sisters ",["Succotash","dish/succotash"]," and corn cakes with cranberries."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Plimoth Patuxet Museums, What's for dinner","url":"https://plimoth.org/for-students/homework-help/whats-for-dinner"},{"name":"Mashpee Wampanoag Tribe, Chef Sherry Pocknett wins Best Chef Northeast","url":"https://mashpeewampanoagtribe-nsn.gov/sherry-james-beard"},{"name":"National Geographic, Sherry Pocknett's fish hash","url":"https://nationalgeographic.com/culture/article/recipe-fish-hash-corn-cake-sherry-pocknett"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"wampanoag-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Nasaump","dish/nasaump"],["Succotash","dish/succotash"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Bell peppers","ingredient/bell-peppers"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Edible salt","ingredient/edible-salt"],["Lima bean","ingredient/lima-bean"],["Sweet corn","ingredient/sweet-corn"],["Tomatoes","ingredient/tomatoes"],["Zea mays Saccharata Group","ingredient/zea-mays-saccharata-group"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4a/Succotash.jpg/500px-Succotash.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"customcabf100","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Succotash.jpg","of":"Succotash"}},"ingredient/paratha":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/paratha","n":"Paratha","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Paratha is a flatbread from the Indian subcontinent, consisting of a wheat dough, typically whole wheat, that is folded and rolled with ghee, forming multiple layers, and shallow fried. Paratha may be stuffed with various fillings, the most common being potato. Paratha is one of the most popular flatbreads in India. The folded, whole-wheat version is prevalent in the Northern Indian subcontinent, and is distinct from South Indian parotta, which uses refined flour. Variations also exist in the cuisines of Myanmar, Mauritius, Malaysia (where it is known as roti canai), and the Caribbean (where it is known as buss-up-shut)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-paratha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paratha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"]]],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine",[["Paratha roll","dish/paratha-roll"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Atta (flour)","ingredient/atta-flour"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1e/Triangle_paratha_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Triangle_paratha_%28cropped%29.JPG","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Nithyasrm","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triangle_paratha_(cropped).JPG"}},"ingredient/lemongrass":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lemongrass","n":"Lemongrass","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 38 dishes across 13 cuisines","intro":[["Cymbopogon, also known as lemongrass, barbed wire grass, silky heads, oily heads, Cochin grass, Malabar grass, citronella grass or fever grass, is a genus of Asian, African, Australian, and tropical island plants in the grass family."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lemongrass"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemongrass"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":13,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Gepuk","dish/gepuk"],["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Konro","dish/konro"],["Laksa","dish/laksa"],["Lemper","dish/lemper"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie ayam","dish/mie-ayam"],["Mi