_Leeds_(30th_March_2013).JPG"}},"ingredient/condensed-milk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/condensed-milk","n":"Condensed milk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 8 dishes across 9 cuisines","intro":[["Condensed milk is cow's milk from which water has been removed (roughly 60% of it). It is most often found with sugar added, in the form of sweetened condensed milk, to the extent that the terms \"condensed milk\" and \"sweetened condensed milk\" are often used interchangeably today. Sweetened condensed milk is a very thick, sweet product, which when canned can last for years without refrigeration if not opened. The product is used in numerous dessert dishes in many countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-condensed-milk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Condensed_milk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":9,"groups":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Beijinho","dish/beijinho"],["Brigadeiro","dish/brigadeiro"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Pumpkin pie","dish/pumpkin-pie"]]],["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine",[["Mikado cake","dish/mikado-cake"]]],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Rabri","dish/rabri"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Teh talua","dish/teh-talua"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Serradura","dish/serradura"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cocoa powder","ingredient/cocoa-powder"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Hagelslag","ingredient/hagelslag"],["Carabao mango","ingredient/carabao-mango"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Graham crackers","ingredient/graham-crackers"],["Ladyfinger (biscuit)","ingredient/ladyfinger-biscuit"],["Mango","ingredient/mango"],["Savoiarde PAT","ingredient/savoiarde-pat"],["Buttercream","ingredient/buttercream"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2f/2016-03-26-Gezuckerte_Kondensmilch-4699.jpg/500px-2016-03-26-Gezuckerte_Kondensmilch-4699.jpg","w":500,"h":751,"by":"Superbass","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016-03-26-Gezuckerte_Kondensmilch-4699.jpg"}},"dish/bunuelo":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bunuelo","n":"Buñuelo","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A buñuelo (Spanish: [buˈɲwelo], alternatively called boñuelo, bimuelo, birmuelo, bermuelo, bumuelo, burmuelo, or bonuelo, is a fried dough fritter found in Spain, Latin America, Philippines, and other regions with a historical connection to Spaniards or Sephardic Jews, including Southwest Europe, the Balkans, Anatolia, where the sweet form is called lokma and the rest pişi, and parts of Asia and North Africa. Buñuelos are traditionally prepared at Christmas, Easter, and Hanukkah. They usually have a filling or a topping. In Mexican cuisine, it is often served with a syrup made with piloncillo."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bunuelo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bu%C3%B1uelo"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Latin American cuisine","cuisine/latin-american-cuisine"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","ref":"site/spainonafork-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafo